<%# locals: (label: "Proceed to Checkout", button_class: "btn btn-primary w-full", collects_postcode: false) %>
<%# Shared checkout button that fires the GA4 begin_checkout dataLayer event on submit.
    Used by the cart drawer and the header dropdown so every checkout entry point is
    instrumented, mirroring the cart-page form (cart_items/_index.html.erb). The form-level
    analytics wiring comes from analytics_checkout_form_data, the single home for that
    contract (it also gates the cost on gtm_enabled?).

    turbo: false keeps this a full navigation to the Stripe-bound checkout_path; the
    dataLayer push happens on submit, before the redirect.

    Strict locals (defaults above) raise a clear error rather than 500-ing on a missing
    local, and give callers sensible defaults.

    Self-guard so the partial is safe in isolation (callers already guard, but a nil/empty
    Current.cart would otherwise crash on total_amount). No cart, no checkout button. Uses
    the memoized line_items_count rather than cart_items.any? to avoid an extra SELECT 1. %>
<% return unless (Current.cart&.line_items_count || 0).positive? %>
<%# Checkout is refused without a delivery destination it can price (shipping is
    baked into the Stripe line items before Stripe collects the address), so with
    no destination this offers something other than a POST into a guaranteed
    rejection. WHICH something depends on whether the caller has a postcode field
    of its own; the condition matches CheckoutsController#deliverable_destination?
    (see the note in cart_items/_checkout.html.erb on why they must agree).

    `collects_postcode` callers (the drawer) disable the button and say what is
    missing, exactly as the cart page does: the field is right there, so sending
    the customer to another page to type something they could type here is a
    detour, and a link styled as a button reads as though checkout were available.

    Everyone else (the navbar dropdown) keeps the link to the cart. That surface
    genuinely cannot collect a postcode, so a disabled button would dead-end the
    main first-time conversion path with nothing to act on. %>
<% if delivery_destination_known?(Current.cart, address_postcode: cart_owner_default_postcode(Current.cart)) %>
  <%= form_with url: checkout_path, method: :post, data: analytics_checkout_form_data(Current.cart) do %>
    <button type="submit" class="<%= button_class %>"><%= label %></button>
  <% end %>
<% elsif collects_postcode %>
  <%# No begin_checkout here, and that is a real cost: the link below reports it
      on click, a disabled button reports nothing, so this surface stops
      contributing that signal while it has no destination. Accepted because the
      cart page's disabled button already behaves this way, and the two surfaces
      disagreeing on the funnel would be worse than both being quiet. %>
  <button type="submit" class="<%= button_class %>" disabled><%= label %></button>
  <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-center text-gray-600" data-test="drawer-checkout-blocked-note">
    <%= checkout_blocked_note %>
  </p>
<% else %>
  <%# Still reports begin_checkout: clicking this IS the customer starting
      checkout, they just have to give a postcode first, so the funnel must not
      lose the event just because the affordance is a link. %>
  <%= link_to label, cart_path, class: button_class,
              data: analytics_checkout_link_data(Current.cart).merge(test: "checkout-needs-postcode") %>
<% end %>
